Make one pull request that adds a --tsv flag to the CLI and documents it. It must meet both of these acceptance lines:
1. "node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV"
2. "README.md documents --tsv"

Steps:
1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. src/parse.js already exports parse(text, sep = ','), so the separator is already a parameter.
2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv contains '--tsv'. If it does, call parse with '\t' as the separator. If it does not, keep the current comma behaviour exactly as it is. Output stays JSON.stringify(...) followed by a newline.
3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input: 'a\tb\n1\t2\n', encoding: 'utf8' }) and asserts the output equals the CSV run's output for 'a,b\n1,2\n' ('[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'). Keep the existing tests unchanged.
4. In README.md, add a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and a sentence saying --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin instead of CSV.
5. Run npm test and make sure every test passes. Also run printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv and printf 'a,b\n1,2\n3,4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js and confirm the two outputs are identical.
6. Commit the changes and open the pull request.